WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.584
Your eyes in weightlessness.

2
00:00:02.624 --> 00:00:06.005
A change that's easy to miss, and worth
catching early.

3
00:00:07.255 --> 00:00:10.564
After a few weeks on board, Aminata
notices something

4
00:00:10.564 --> 00:00:13.124
odd: her checklist looks blurry up close.

5
00:00:13.164 --> 00:00:15.202
At first, she blames the screen.

6
00:00:16.102 --> 00:00:18.901
Remember the fluid that moves up toward
the head?

7
00:00:18.901 --> 00:00:21.129
It also presses on the back of the eye.

8
00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:26.081
The back of the eyeball flattens slightly,
and the optic nerve can swell.

9
00:00:26.981 --> 00:00:31.742
About seven in ten astronauts on long
missions show changes like these.

10
00:00:32.642 --> 00:00:37.724
That's why your eyes are checked regularly
on board, with eye scans and vision tests.

11
00:00:37.764 --> 00:00:41.742
Keiko compares each result with the ones
taken before the flight.

12
00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:46.090
If your near vision changes, don't wait:
tell Keiko.

13
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.389
Adjustable glasses are on board.

14
00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:52.330
Caught early, these changes can be
followed closely.

